a heterogeneous graph transformer trained on data assembled using the Indra tool 51-53 . For biomedical text, we utilized embeddings from Open AI's text-embedding-ada-002 accessed via GenePT 54 . Each gene's embedding was averaged across the up- and down-regulated gene lists to create representations of gene lists. [truncated]","original_chars":1652},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Methods","heading_level":2,"line_start":301,"line_end":430,"text":"### Data collection and processing\n\nWe aggregated omics data from experiments deposited in public repositories (see Table 2 for a full list of datasets included in the model's training). To conform with the P3GPT-enforced prompt structure, omics measurements were reduced to lists of differentially expressed genes for RNAseq and expression profiling through array experiments, differentially methylated TSS1500 regions for RRBS and methylation profiling through array experiments, or differentially abundant proteins for proteome experiments.\n\nFor LINCS, to take one example, we used level-5 data representing DEG signatures as defined by the consortium. Only LINCS chemical perturbation experiments were collected for the model's training. We defined resources that did not report results as lists of features differentiating case and control cohorts using DESeq2 for expression data 49 . For methylation data, we used custom scripts to calculate the average /i80-value among all CpG sites observed in an experiment for TSS1500 regions, regardless of the platform.\n\nData from three species were included in P3GPT's training: Homo sapiens , Mus musculus , and Macaca mulatta . For H. sapiens , we mapped any gene representations encountered onto NCBI gene symbols, ignoring transcript variants. Only protein-coding genes were considered, yielding a background set of 25,332 unique genes. For M. musculus and M. mulatta , all genes were denoted as the gene symbol of their human homolog with the highest similarity, as described on ENSEMBL. [truncated]","original_chars":18713}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001187.md"}]
./data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0162.stderr.log:123:{"results":[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001187","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper presents P3GPT as a new model, not a wrapper, and explicitly describes a decoder-only LLaMA-like transformer. It is trained on over 1.2 million omics observations across species, tissues, and modalities, which supports foundation-model-like breadth.","evidence_snippet":"\"primary component is based on the MPT architecture, a decoder-only LLaMA-like transformer model\"; \"we aggregated... data amounted to over 1.2 million observations\"; \"novel approach to training domain-specialized language models\"; \"additional modalities-biomedical texts and KGs\"; \"cross-source harmonization\"; \"can be integrated into the framework\"; \"supporting a range of research problems\"; \"training set tokens\"; \"data from three species were included\"; \"gene expression, DNA methylation, and proteomics\"; \"age prediction to chemical screening\"; \"effectively fusing diverse knowledge sources\"; \"transformer block (TB)\"; \"platform-agnostic\"; \"open-sourced MosaicML platform, MPT\"; \"bio\"; \"omics\"; \"text\"; \"knowledge graphs\"; \"P3GPT\"; \"MPT\"; \"LLaMA-like\"; \"decoder-only\"; \"41 million parameters\"; \"Chinchilla scaling law\"; \"trained on data assembled using the Indra tool\"; \"open AI's text-embedding-ada-002\"; \"GenePT\"; \"instructions added to inform P3GPT\"; \"crossentropy loss function plateaued\"; \"up- and downregulated gene lists\"; \"multi-species multi-omics multi-tissue transformer\"; \"novel approach\"; \"multimodal\"; \"broad applicability\"; \"utility in a real-life research project\""},{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001187","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper presents P3GPT as a new model, not a wrapper, and explicitly describes a decoder-only LLaMA-like transformer. It is trained on over 1.2 million omics observations across species, tissues, and modalities, which supports foundation-model-like breadth.","evidence_snippet":"\"primary component is based on the MPT architecture, a decoder-only LLaMA-like transformer model\"; \"we aggregated... data amounted to over 1.2 million observations\"; \"novel approach to training domain-specialized language models\"; \"additional modalities-biomedical texts and KGs\"; \"cross-source harmonization\"; \"can be integrated into the framework\"; \"supporting a range of research problems\"; \"training set tokens\"; \"data from three species were included\"; \"gene expression, DNA methylation, and proteomics\"; \"age prediction to chemical screening\"; \"effectively fusing diverse knowledge sources\"; \"transformer block (TB)\"; \"platform-agnostic\"; \"open-sourced MosaicML platform, MPT\"; \"bio\"; \"omics\"; \"text\"; \"knowledge graphs\"; \"P3GPT\"; \"MPT\"; \"LLaMA-like\"; \"decoder-only\"; \"41 million parameters\"; \"Chinchilla scaling law\"; \"trained on data assembled using the Indra tool\"; \"open AI's text-embedding-ada-002\"; \"GenePT\"; \"instructions added to inform P3GPT\"; \"crossentropy loss function plateaued\"; \"up- and downregulated gene lists\"; \"multi-species multi-omics multi-tissue transformer\"; \"novel approach\"; \"multimodal\"; \"broad applicability\"; \"utility in a real-life research project\""}]}
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:33:The large data volume and diversity of X-Atlas/Pisces enabled us to develop X-Cell, a diffusion language model pre-trained across diverse cell lines and perturbations in Pisces (Figure 1B). Because perturbational cascades are constrained by underlying regulatory networks, we designed X-Cell to incorporate multi-modal biological priors directly into the generative process through cross-attention (Figure 1B). These priors are encoded as pre-trained embeddings derived from large language models (GenePT) [19], protein language models (ESM-2) [38], interaction networks (STRING) [40, 41], cancer dependency maps (DepMap) [42], morphological profiling data (JUMP-Cell Painting) [43], and single-cell foundation models [8]. Furthermore, X-Cell models the transcriptomic shift from a control state to a perturbed state via an iterative diffusion process. Predictions are progressively refined across intermediate steps through remasking (Figure 1C). To examine scaling effects, we trained two model variants: X-Cell and X-CellUltra. X-Cell-Ultra contains 4.9 billion parameters, enabling perturbation modeling at unprecedented scale (Figure 1D). To systematically evaluate performance, we partitioned X-Atlas/Pisces into three taskspecific regimes: (1) stable cell lines for architecture validation, (2) Jurkat resting and stimulated states for context generalization, and (3) the iPSC Multi-Diff dataset for zero-shot cell type generalization (Section 4.1.7).
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:147:- **D**: pie chart labeled **Cross-Attention Contribution** with sources such as **STRING, ESM-2, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting**, plus a line plot for metric sensitivity to knowledge-source randomization.  
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:158:To elucidate the mechanistic basis of this performance, we analyzed the model's cross-attention scores, hypothesizing that external knowledge bases provide critical, context-specific biological priors during inference. Specifically, during the simulation of CD3E perturbation in active Jurkat cells, we observed a marked increase in the attention weights assigned to ESM-2, GenePT, and DepMap embeddings. This focus is biologically corroborated by the significant enrichment of the top 1,000 genes prioritized by these modalities in the KEGG TCR signaling pathway (hsa04660) [56]. These results suggest that XCell dynamically leverages relevant external knowledge to refine its predictions within specific biological contexts (Supplementary Figure 5).
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:260:GenePT (text embeddings). GenePT embeddings encode semantic representations of gene function by applying large language model text embeddings to NCBI gene summaries and related annotations. These embeddings capture functional relationships between genes learned from biomedical literature and textual gene descriptions [19]. We used the GenePT embedding release 1 generated using the text-embedding-3-large model, specifically GenePT gene protein embedding model 3 text.pickle .
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:314:where the context C pert ∈ R 6 × d consists of six prior knowledge embeddings associated with the perturbed gene. These embeddings are derived from ESM-2 protein language model [38], STRING interaction networks [41], GenePT gene representations [19], DepMap genetic dependency profiles [42], JUMP-Cell Painting morphological features [43], and the gene identity embedding itself (i.e., initialized from scGPT and updated during training in X-Cell). Because the prior knowledge embeddings have different dimensionalities, each embedding is projected to dimension d through a two-layer MLP with LeakyReLU activation and LayerNorm (Section A.1.2). The prior knowledge autoencoders are optimized with a reconstruction loss (Section 4.1.3). Each cross-attention block additionally includes a SwiGLU feed-forward sublayer with a Pre-LN residual connection.
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:498:Cross Attention Interpretability. To characterize how individual perturbation conditions modulate attention to prior biological knowledge, we extracted cross-attention weights from the final crossattention layer of the X-Cell model. Our approach utilized the model's cross-attention mechanism, wherein the input gene expression sequence attends to a curated set of six prior knowledge embeddings derived from ESM-2, STRING, GenePT, DepMap, CellPainting, and scGPT. As the cross attention weights for each gene token are softmax normalized across these six embeddings, they provide a quantitative measure of the relative importance assigned to each knowledge source. We used the same 32 control cells from benchmark and performed inference across 380 test perturbations in the ReplogleNadig HepG2 test set. We performed single-step prediction for simplicity, without iterative diffusion. During extraction, attention weights were averaged across heads and genes in each cell, and aggregated across cells for each perturbation.
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:526:Subsequent work incorporated prior biological knowledge to improve extrapolation to unseen perturbations. Graph-based models such as GEARS explicitly integrate gene-gene interaction networks to enhance generalization to unseen and combinatorial perturbations [18]. Other approaches, including PRESAGE, TxPert, and GenePT, leverage pre-trained gene embeddings or structured priors derived from large biological corpora to inform perturbation modeling [19-21]. These knowledge-informed strategies provide evidence that incorporating gene regulatory or interaction structure can improve performance on unseen perturbations. However, their predictive formulation primarily captures conditional mean responses relative to control profiles and is commonly evaluated on pseudobulk summaries or deterministic reconstructions, rather than explicitly modeling the full single-cell distribution of perturbed states.
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:688:where each MLP s : R d s → R d is a two-layer network with LeakyReLU activation and LayerNorm output. The six tokens form the cross-attention context C pert = [ c ESM ; c STRING ; c GenePT ; c DepMap ; c CP ; c gene ] ∈
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:774:where S = { ESM , STRING , GenePT , DepMap , CP } .
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:1038:- GenePT [19]: z GenePT ∈ R 3072 , multi-modal gene representation from LLM
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:1342:Panel A: clustered heatmap titled “Attention weights per perturbation,” with rows for modalities `ESM2`, `StringDB`, `GenePT`, `DepMap`, `CellPainting`, and `scGPT`; columns are `380 perturbations`. A red color scale indicates attention weight, approximately 0.00 to 0.32. A dendrogram clusters perturbations across the top.
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:1344:Panel B: bar chart titled “Total significant pathways per modality,” y-axis “Total significant pathways,” x-axis “Modality.” Bars show StringDB highest at 94, scGPT 80, GenePT 44, ESM2 42, DepMap 38, and CellPainting 13.
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:1360:Panel B: Bar plot titled “Change in Attention Scores of TCR Signaling Pathway Genes.” Y-axis shows relative change in contribution, arbitrary scale. Methods on the x-axis include StringDB, scGPT, CellPainting, ESM2, GenePT, and DepMap. GenePT, ESM2, and DepMap show positive increases, while StringDB, scGPT, and CellPainting show decreases.
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:1362:Panel C: Bar plot titled “Over-representation of the Top 1,000 Genes in TCR Signaling Pathway.” Y-axis is odds ratio. Bars compare GenePT, ESM2, DepMap, CellPainting, scGPT, and StringDB, with a dashed reference line at odds ratio 1. Legend indicates dark outlines for p-value < 0.05 and light outlines for p-value ≥ 0.05. GenePT, ESM2, and DepMap show stronger enrichment than the other methods.
./data/docling_include_vlm_52_2026-07-10_nolimits/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003517.md:1364:Supplementary Figure 5 External knowledge bases provide context-specific information to X-Cell. (A) Schematic of the X-Cell architecture, which employs cross-attention between gene embeddings (initialized via scGPT) and external knowledge bases including DepMap, STRING, CellPainting, ESM-2, and GenePT. To evaluate the contribution of relevant knowledge from these sources, we analyzed cross-attention scores during inference of the CD3E perturbation (a known T cell inactivating perturbation) among activated Jurkat cells. We compared the distribution of attention scores for genes within the KEGG TCR Signaling pathway (hsa04660) against the global distribution of all genes. An upward shift in scores indicates the increased importance of these knowledge sources for this specific pathway. (B) Bar plots illustrating the increase in attention scores for ESM-2, GenePT, and DepMap embeddings within the KEGG TCR signaling pathway genes. (C) Over-representation of the top 1,000 genes with the highest attention scores for each modality within the KEGG TCR signaling pathway. Bars represent the Odds Ratio (OR) from a one-sided Fisher's exact test; black outlines denote statistical significance ( p &lt; 0 . 05).
